    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_121'), 'Ten successful requests to POST /api/submissions from a short smoke test, sorted by duration (ms):

180, 190, 195, 205, 210, 220, 240, 260, 300, 4100

The course uses nearest-rank percentiles: rank = ceil(p / 100 * n), value = the rank-th smallest sample.

Statement: "The p95 of these samples is 300 ms, so 95% of the submissions were accepted within 300 ms."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. With n = 10, rank = ceil(0.95 * 10) = ceil(9.5) = 10, so p95 is the 10th value: 4,100 ms. Only 9 of 10 requests (90%) finished within 300 ms; claiming 95% would need at least 9.5 of them. The common mistake is to round the rank down, or to use int(p / 100 * n) as a 0-based index without checking where it lands, and read the 9th value. It also shows a limit of small samples: with 10 samples, p95 and p99 are both simply the maximum, so a single slow request decides them. Report p99 only from a run with enough samples.', 1, 5
