run   change                                     mean     p50      p99
A     before                                     180 ms    62 ms   2,400 ms
B     400 ms timeout on the slow ranking query,  210 ms   175 ms     410 ms
      then a precomputed ranking table

Statement: "For learners, run B is better at the tail even though its mean is higher: in run B, 99% of the requests finished within 410 ms, against 2,400 ms in run A."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The p99 is a statement about requests: in run B, at least 99% of the 12,000 requests (11,880 of them) took 410 ms or less, while in run A the slowest 1% took 2,400 ms or more. The mean mixes everything into one number: run A''s many fast requests hide its slow ones, and in run B the typical request got slower (p50 175 ms) while the worst ones got much faster. The misconception is to compare runs by their mean: it can rise while the experience of the slowest learners improves a lot, and it can fall while it gets worse. Compare the percentiles you have a target for (here p95 and p99), with enough samples behind them.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
