WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_211'), 'Next run, same endpoint (k6 option names; check current docs):
executor: ''ramping-arrival-rate'', startRate: 0, timeUnit: ''1s'', preAllocatedVUs: 800,
stages: [
  { duration: ''5m'', target: 340 },
  { duration: ''15m'', target: 340 },
  { duration: ''2m'', target: 0 },
]

Grafana panel, per minute of the run:
 minute | arrival rate | p95 submit acceptance
 1      |  34/s        | 140 ms
 3      | 170/s        | 210 ms
 5      | 306/s        | 690 ms
 6-10   | 340/s        | 1.4 s -> 1.9 s
 11-20  | 340/s        | 2.0 s -> 2.6 s (rising each minute)
 21-22  | 255/s, 85/s  | 900 ms, 160 ms
k6 end-of-test summary, whole run: http_req_duration p(95)=1.84s

Statement: "Judged against the target of 2 s at p95 for 340 submissions per second, this run fails, although the end-of-test summary reports p95 = 1.84 s."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The end-of-test p95 mixes every request of the run: about 51,000 from the ramp-up (average 170/s for 5 minutes) and 20,400 from the ramp-down ran at lower rates and were mostly fast, and they pull the p95 under 2 s. The target is about 340/s, so the number to compare comes from the 15-minute hold only, and there p95 rises every minute and passes 2 s from minute 11. A latency that keeps rising at a constant arrival rate means a queue is growing: the system is not keeping up with 340/s. The misconception is to trust the whole-run summary of a profile that includes ramps.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_211') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
