WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_321'), 'Evidence A - k6 end-of-test summary, exam rehearsal on staging (2 API instances). Profile: 160 submissions/s for 2 hours with a deadline spike of 340/s for 80 s at 1h55m (the exam-day forecast).

  http_req_duration..: avg=120.4ms min=6.2ms med=41.3ms max=6.14s p(90)=58.9ms p(95)=74.0ms p(99)=3.64s
  http_req_failed....: 0.00%  0 out of 1,166,412
  http_reqs..........: 1,166,412  161.8/s

The weekly report took one line from this run: "POST /api/submissions: average 120 ms".

Statement: "The 120 ms average and the 6 s waits do not contradict each other: p(99) is 3.64 s, so more than 11,000 requests took longer than 3.6 s, and they are only about 1% of a run that was calm for almost two hours."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. 1% of 1,166,412 requests is about 11,700 requests at or above 3.64 s, up to the 6.14 s maximum. Even at 3.6 s each they add at least 11,700 x 3.6 s / 1,166,412 = about 36 ms to the average, and the rest of the slow spike requests add more; the other 98% at about 41 ms make up the rest of the 120 ms. The misconception is that an average close to normal means nobody had a bad time: an average spreads a short, severe incident over hours of quiet traffic.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_321') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
