  http_req_duration..: avg=120.4ms min=6.2ms med=41.3ms max=6.14s p(90)=58.9ms p(95)=74.0ms p(99)=3.64s
  http_req_failed....: 0.00%  0 out of 1,166,412
  http_reqs..........: 1,166,412  161.8/s

The weekly report took one line from this run: "POST /api/submissions: average 120 ms".

Statement: "p(95)=74.0ms shows that at least 95% of the submissions sent during the deadline spike got a response within 74 ms."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The percentiles in A are computed over the whole two-hour run. The 80 s spike plus the time to drain its queue is only about 2-3% of all requests, so the whole-run p95 is decided by the calm part and says nothing about the spike. Split the run by time (or tag the spike as its own scenario) and compute the percentiles of the spike alone; B shows its p95 at 5.83 s. The misconception is that a percentile describes every period of the run; it describes the population it was computed on.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_321') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
