span                              at 160/s          deadline spike
POST /api/submissions             41 ms / 68 ms     3.05 s / 5.83 s
  auth + validate                  2 ms /  4 ms      2 ms /  4 ms
  db.pool.acquire                  0 ms /  1 ms     3.01 s / 5.79 s
  tx: INSERT submissions           4 ms /  9 ms      5 ms / 11 ms
  tx: PUT source to storage       27 ms / 44 ms     28 ms / 46 ms
  tx: enqueue judge job            1 ms /  2 ms      1 ms /  3 ms
  tx: COMMIT                       2 ms /  4 ms      2 ms /  5 ms

Evidence C - metrics:
                                  at 160/s          deadline spike
submissions accepted per second   160               316
API CPU (2 instances, average)    21%               40%
DB pool per instance (max 6)      3.0 in use        6 in use all the time
requests waiting for the pool     0                 grows to 1,920
mean connection hold time         38 ms             38 ms
PostgreSQL CPU                    15%               31%

Statement: "In the spike, almost all of the 5.83 s p95 is time spent waiting in db.pool.acquire, which is also why API CPU can stay at 40%: a request that waits for a connection uses almost no CPU."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Every span except db.pool.acquire stays within a few milliseconds of its value at 160/s; the acquire wait goes from 1 ms to 5.79 s at p95. A request waiting in the pool''s queue holds memory and a slot, but no CPU, so the CPU graph cannot show this kind of saturation. The misconception is "CPU at 40% means there is headroom": CPU is one resource of many, and the first one to saturate here is the connection pool.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_321') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
