POST /api/submissions             41 ms / 68 ms     3.05 s / 5.83 s
  auth + validate                  2 ms /  4 ms      2 ms /  4 ms
  db.pool.acquire                  0 ms /  1 ms     3.01 s / 5.79 s
  tx: INSERT submissions           4 ms /  9 ms      5 ms / 11 ms
  tx: PUT source to storage       27 ms / 44 ms     28 ms / 46 ms
  tx: enqueue judge job            1 ms /  2 ms      1 ms /  3 ms
  tx: COMMIT                       2 ms /  4 ms      2 ms /  5 ms

Evidence C - metrics:
                                  at 160/s          deadline spike
submissions accepted per second   160               316
API CPU (2 instances, average)    21%               40%
DB pool per instance (max 6)      3.0 in use        6 in use all the time
requests waiting for the pool     0                 grows to 1,920
mean connection hold time         38 ms             38 ms
PostgreSQL CPU                    15%               31%

Statement: "With 12 connections in total, each held for 38 ms on average, the API can accept at most about 316 submissions per second, so at 340/s the queue for the pool must grow for as long as the spike lasts."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. By Little''s Law, connections in use = throughput x hold time, so the most the pool can serve is 12 / 0.038 s = about 316 submissions per second, which is what C measured during the spike. At 340/s, about 24 more requests arrive every second than the pool can serve, and over 80 s that is the 1,920 waiting requests in C: each new request waits behind all of them, about 1,920 / 316 = 6 s. The misconception is to look for a slow component; at full utilisation the wait comes from the arrival rate exceeding the service rate, even when every step is fast.', 5, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_321') AND order_index = 5)
    RETURNING id
)
