-- challenge chal_coding_qpr_322 (fix_bug)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_qpr_322', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'qa-performance-reliability'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'qa-performance-reliability') AND order_index = 3 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-qpr-3-2' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'fix-the-bottleneck-finder', 'Find the First Bottleneck', 'CodeDev runs a step load test of POST /api/submissions before every exam: the offered rate rises in steps, and for each step a script collects, per resource, its average utilization and the time each request waited for it. tools/perf/bottleneck.py should name the first bottleneck: the resource where requests started to wait first. On the exam-day step test it reported db_pool at 340 req/s, the last step, although the p95 pool wait was already above the limit at 280 req/s, and it also lists judge_workers as saturated, although no submit request ever waits for a judge worker (the judge runs asynchronously). Fix find_first_bottleneck so that it follows the rule below.

Input (stdin, JSON): {"wait_limit_ms": L, "steps": [{"rate": offered req/s, "resources": {"<name>": {"util": 0..1, "waits_ms": [wait samples in ms]}, ...}}, ...]}. Steps can be in any order. A step whose rate is not a number greater than 0 (missing, a string, a bool, 0 or negative) is ignored. A resource can have no "util", and no or an empty "waits_ms" (no request waited for it).

Rule:
- A resource is saturated at a step when the p95 of its waits_ms at that step is greater than L. Utilization is not part of the rule: a resource can be busy all the time without making requests wait, and an average utilization hides waits.
- Its from_rate is the lowest rate at which it is saturated, even if it is not saturated at some higher step.
- "saturated" lists every saturated resource as {"resource", "from_rate", "p95_wait_ms"} (p95_wait_ms = the p95 wait at from_rate, rounded with round(v, 1)), sorted by from_rate, then by the higher p95 wait (unrounded), then by name.
- first_bottleneck and at_rate are the resource and from_rate of the first entry, or null and null when nothing is saturated.

Percentiles: nearest-rank on sorted samples, rank = ceil(p/100 * n), value = sorted[rank - 1]; an empty list has no percentile (the starter''s percentile function already does this).

Output: {"first_bottleneck": name or null, "at_rate": rate or null, "saturated": [...]}. Example: {"first_bottleneck": "db_pool", "at_rate": 280, "saturated": [{"resource": "db_pool", "from_rate": 280, "p95_wait_ms": 140}]}.', 'open', 'hard'::difficulty_question, 'fix_bug', 40, true, false)
