    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_511'), 'Objective: 99.5% of valid POST /api/submissions requests are good (202 within 2 s), rolling 28 days.
Last 28 days: 12,400,000 valid submit requests.
Exam-day forecast: 340 submissions/s at peak, confirmed by the load test plan.

Scenario under discussion: a bad deploy makes every submit fail for 2 minutes at the exam-day peak.

Statement: "Those 2 minutes alone would spend more than half of the 28-day error budget."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The budget counted in requests is 0.005 x 12,400,000 = 62,000 bad requests; half of it is 31,000. Two minutes at 340/s are 340 x 120 = 40,800 failed requests, about 66% of the budget. Converted to time at average traffic the budget looks like 201.6 minutes for 28 days, which makes a 2-minute failure seem small; that is the misconception the statement tests. At peak each minute contains far more requests than an average minute, so the request count, not the minutes, is what the SLI and the budget measure.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_qpr_511') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
